        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a drill and an impact driver?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A drill applies steady rotation through a three-jaw chuck and has an adjustable clutch that limits torque. An impact driver adds rapid rotational hammer blows through a quarter-inch hex socket, delivering far more turning force in bursts with almost no twisting reaction felt at your wrist.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which should I buy first, a drill or an impact driver?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A drill, in almost every case. It is the more versatile of the two: it drills holes, which an impact driver cannot do well, and it drives screws adequately. An impact driver is a specialist that does one job superbly and several jobs badly.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can an impact driver drill holes?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can with hex-shank bits, but not well. There is no chuck for round-shank bits, no clutch, and the impact action makes precise starts difficult. It is workable for rough holes in wood and a poor choice for anything requiring accuracy or a clean edge.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a drill drive long screws?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, up to a point. Beyond about three inches in softwood, or in dense material, the drill starts twisting in your hand and may stall or strip the screw head. That wrist-wrenching moment is exactly the problem an impact driver was invented to solve.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need both a drill and an impact driver?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not to start, but most people who build anything end up with both, which is why they are so often sold as a pair. The combination lets you keep a bit in one and a driver in the other, which saves more time than either tool's speed advantage.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is an impact driver so loud?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The noise is the mechanism working. Inside, a hammer is repeatedly striking an anvil thousands of times a minute to deliver rotational blows. That metallic chattering is unavoidable and loud enough that hearing protection is sensible for sustained use.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use regular drill bits in an impact driver?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only bits with a quarter-inch hex shank, since that is the only shape the collet accepts. Standard round-shank drill bits will not fit. Any bit used in an impact driver should also be rated for impact use, because ordinary bits can shatter under the hammering.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will an impact driver strip screws?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can, because most have no clutch to stop them. The tool will happily drive a screw straight through the surface, snap it, or chew out the head if you keep the trigger down. Control comes from your trigger finger and from listening for the impacts to change.",
