    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sold side by side, in matching cases, on the same batteries, these two tools look like variations on a theme. They are not. One is a precision instrument with a torque limiter; the other is a controlled hammer that happens to turn things, and using either for the other's work is how people strip screws and snap bits.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you can only own one, own a drill. It makes holes, which an impact driver does badly, and it drives screws well enough for most household work. An impact driver is the specialist you add when driving fasteners becomes a significant part of what you do.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Division of Labour</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drill: holes, precision, control, anything that must not be over-tightened.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Impact driver: long screws, lag bolts, decking, framing, structural screws, and any fastener that would otherwise twist the tool out of your hand.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The reason they are so often sold as a two-tool kit is not upselling. Once you own both, you stop swapping bits constantly — a drill bit stays in one and a driver bit in the other — and that alone saves more time on a project than either tool's raw speed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mechanism" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Each One Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            A drill-driver is straightforward. A motor turns a gearbox, the gearbox turns a three-jaw chuck, and the chuck grips whatever round or hex shank you put in it. Between the gearbox and the chuck sits an adjustable clutch — the numbered collar behind the chuck — which slips and stops transmitting torque once resistance passes a set threshold. Rotation is smooth and continuous, and the tool applies steady force in one direction.
          </p>

          <p>
            An impact driver replaces that smooth transmission with something considerably more violent. It drives a quarter-inch hex collet through a mechanism containing a spring-loaded hammer and an anvil. Under light load it turns like an ordinary driver, but once resistance rises, the hammer disengages, spins up, and strikes the anvil rotationally — thousands of times a minute, each blow delivering a burst of torque far beyond what the motor alone could produce.
          </p>

          <p>
            That mechanism explains everything about how the tool behaves. It is loud, because metal is striking metal continuously. It is enormously strong for its size, because each blow is an impulse rather than sustained force. And it produces very little reaction in your hand, because the twisting force is being absorbed by the mechanism rather than transmitted back through the handle.
          </p>

          <p>
            One important clarification, since the names cause endless confusion: an impact driver is not a hammer drill. A hammer drill delivers blows <em>forward</em>, along the axis of the bit, to chip masonry. An impact driver delivers blows <em>rotationally</em>, to turn fasteners. They are different mechanisms for entirely different jobs, as our guide to <a href="/hammer-drill-vs-drill-driver/">hammer drills and drill drivers</a> explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Side-by-Side
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
