                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Impact Driver</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bit holder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Three-jaw chuck, any shank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4" hex collet only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Torque</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate and steady</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two to three times higher, in bursts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clutch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adjustable, protects the work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually none</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kickback</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Significant on big fasteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost none</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Noise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very loud under impact</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Size and weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longer head</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shorter, lighter, better in tight spots</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drilling holes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Its primary purpose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor — rough and imprecise</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Delicate work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good, thanks to the clutch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor — easy to overdrive</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that the impact driver wins on exactly two dimensions — torque and compactness — and loses or ties everywhere else. Those two happen to matter enormously for a specific and common job, which is why the tool exists at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reaction" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Your Wrist Notices
          </h2>

          <p>
            The least obvious advantage of an impact driver is the one people appreciate most after a day's work, and it comes down to Newton's third law.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a drill applies steady rotational force to a stubborn screw, an equal and opposite force acts on the tool — and therefore on your hand. Drive a four-inch structural screw into dense timber with a drill and the tool tries to rotate in your grip, sometimes hard enough to hurt. On a large auger bit that grabs, a powerful drill can genuinely injure a wrist.
          </p>

          <p>
            An impact driver sidesteps this. Because the torque is delivered as thousands of tiny rotational blows rather than continuous force, the reaction is absorbed inside the mechanism and dissipated between blows. You feel a rapid vibration and a lot of noise, but almost no twisting. This is why someone can drive deck screws all afternoon with an impact driver and be tired but not sore.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also changes how you can work. Because there is no reaction to brace against, an impact driver can be used one-handed, overhead, or at an awkward angle where you could not hold a drill firmly enough to resist the kickback. On a ladder, that is a safety benefit rather than merely a convenience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-task" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Tool by Task
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drilling any hole, in anything</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Assembling flat-pack furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill, clutch set low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hanging a door or fitting hinges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill — precision matters</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Building a deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impact driver</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Framing, ledgers, lag bolts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impact driver</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fixing plasterboard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill with clutch, or a dedicated screwgun</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Driving into hardwood or old timber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impact driver, after a pilot hole</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Anything into soft or thin material</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill — the clutch saves the workpiece</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Working in a tight corner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impact driver — shorter head</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Removing a seized or rusted screw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Impact driver — impacts break it free</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The last row deserves a mention because it is an underrated party trick. The hammering action that drives fasteners in is equally effective at shocking a corroded screw loose, in much the way a mechanic's impact wrench frees a stubborn nut. A drill will often just round out the head.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clutch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Clutch Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            The numbered collar behind a drill's chuck is a mechanical torque limiter, and it is the single feature that makes a drill safe for delicate work. Set it low and the drill slips with an audible clicking once the screw meets a set resistance, so it cannot be driven any deeper.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is what stops a screw head sinking through the face of a cabinet door, snapping a brass screw, stripping the threads in a soft material, or crushing a hinge into the timber. When a job requires screws to finish flush and consistent, the clutch does the thinking for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most impact drivers have no clutch at all. They deliver everything they have until you release the trigger, which means the only limit is your judgement. Drive a screw into softwood with an impact driver and it can vanish below the surface in an instant, taking a chunk of the material with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skilled users manage this by trigger control and by ear, since the change in impact sound tells you when the fastener has seated. Some newer impact drivers add electronic modes that ease off automatically for small fasteners or self-tapping screws, which genuinely helps. It is still not a substitute for a clutch, and it is the main reason a drill remains the better first tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Bits Are Not Interchangeable
          </h2>

          <p>
            This catches people out and costs them bits. A drill's three-jaw chuck grips anything with a round or hex shank, so it accepts the entire universe of drill bits, hole saws, spade bits, wire brushes and driver bits.
          </p>

          <p>
            An impact driver accepts only quarter-inch hex shanks, because that is the shape its collet is made for. Standard round-shank twist bits simply will not fit. Hex-shank drill bits do exist and are sold precisely for this purpose, but they are a limited range compared with what a chuck can hold.
          </p>

          <p>
            More importantly, bits used in an impact driver should be rated for impact. Ordinary hardened driver bits are brittle, and the repeated hammering will snap them — sometimes sending fragments flying. Impact-rated bits are made from a slightly softer, tougher steel designed to flex rather than shatter, and they are usually marked and sold specifically as impact-ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rule when buying accessories: impact-rated bits work perfectly well in a drill, but ordinary bits do not survive long in an impact driver. If you own both tools, buying impact-rated across the board simplifies the drawer and costs very little more. Eye protection matters here too, since a snapping bit is the most likely way this tool causes an injury.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a first purchase, buy a good drill-driver on a battery platform you are happy to live with. It handles every hole and most fasteners a household encounters, and the clutch keeps you out of trouble while you learn what the tool can do. Our guides to the <a href="/best-cordless-drills/">best cordless drills</a> and <a href="/best-cordless-drill-for-home-use/">drills for home use</a> cover the models worth considering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add an impact driver when a project demands it — a deck, a fence, a garden building, a floor — or when you notice yourself fighting the drill on long screws. At that point the combo kit maths usually favours buying the pair together, since the second tool in a kit costs far less than it does alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you buy, the battery platform matters more over a decade than either tool does. Batteries and chargers are where the money accumulates, and switching brands later means abandoning all of it — a point our comparison of <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee and DeWalt</a> and our <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/">battery guide</a> both dwell on for good reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            On motors, the same logic applies as elsewhere in the cordless world: brushless is worth it if you use the tools regularly and less so if they live in a cupboard. Impact drivers benefit more than most, because they work in intense bursts and gain useful electronic modes — see <a href="/brushless-vs-brushed-drill/">brushless versus brushed</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">What About Impact Wrenches and Hammer Drills?</h2>
        <p>
          Two neighbouring tools cause confusion in the aisle. An impact wrench works on the same rotational-hammer principle as an impact driver, but has a square drive for sockets and produces vastly more torque — it is for wheel nuts, structural bolts and machinery, not for wood screws. Buying one to drive fasteners is overkill and hard to control.
        </p>
        <p>
          A hammer drill is a different mechanism entirely, delivering blows forward along the bit to chip masonry while the bit rotates. It is a drill first and can usually switch the hammering off, which makes a combi drill a sensible single-tool choice for a house with brick or block walls.
        </p>
        <p>
          The naming is genuinely unhelpful, so the reliable test is to ask which direction the blows go. Forward, into the material, means masonry drilling. Rotationally, around the axis, means driving fasteners. Everything else follows from that.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Impact Driver as a First and Only Tool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It cannot take round-shank drill bits and has no clutch, so it drills badly and overdrives fasteners in soft material. Impressive torque is no substitute for versatility when you own exactly one power tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Ordinary Bits in an Impact Driver</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard driver bits are hardened and brittle, and repeated hammering snaps them — occasionally throwing fragments. Impact-rated bits are made to flex instead, and they work fine in a drill too, so buying them across the board is simplest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving Delicate Fasteners With an Impact Driver</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With no clutch, a screw can disappear below the surface in an instant, taking material with it. Cabinet work, hinges, brass screws and anything into thin or soft stock want a drill with the clutch set appropriately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fighting a Drill Instead of Reaching for the Right Tool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a drill is twisting hard in your hand on long screws, that is not a technique problem to push through — it is the exact condition an impact driver was designed for, and forcing it risks stripped heads and a wrenched wrist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Pilot Hole Because the Tool Is Strong Enough</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An impact driver will happily drive a screw into hardwood without a pilot and split the board doing it. Torque removes the resistance you would otherwise feel as a warning, so pilot holes matter more with this tool, not less.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a drill and an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A drill applies steady rotation through a three-jaw chuck and has an adjustable clutch limiting torque. An impact driver adds rapid rotational hammer blows through a quarter-inch hex collet, delivering far more turning force in bursts with almost no reaction at your wrist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which should I buy first, a drill or an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A drill, in almost every case. It is the more versatile tool: it drills holes, which an impact driver does poorly, and it drives screws adequately. An impact driver is a specialist that does one job superbly and several jobs badly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an impact driver drill holes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With hex-shank bits, yes, but not well. There is no chuck for round shanks, no clutch, and the impact action makes precise starts hard. Acceptable for rough holes in wood; poor for anything needing accuracy or a clean edge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a drill drive long screws?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Up to a point. Beyond about three inches in softwood, or in dense material, the drill twists in your hand and may stall or strip the head. That moment is precisely the problem an impact driver was invented to solve.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need both a drill and an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to start, but most people who build anything end up with both, which is why they are sold as pairs. Keeping a drill bit in one and a driver bit in the other saves more time than either tool's speed advantage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is an impact driver so loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The noise is the mechanism working — a hammer striking an anvil thousands of times a minute to deliver rotational blows. It is unavoidable and loud enough that hearing protection is sensible for sustained use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use regular drill bits in an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only quarter-inch hex shanks, since that is all the collet takes — standard round-shank bits will not fit. Bits used in an impact driver should also be impact-rated, because ordinary hardened bits can shatter under the hammering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an impact driver strip screws?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, since most have no clutch. The tool will drive a screw through the surface, snap it, or chew the head out if you hold the trigger down. Control comes from your finger and from hearing the impacts change as it seats.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

