        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A drill and an impact driver are not competing versions of the same tool. The drill rotates smoothly through a chuck that takes any bit and a clutch that limits torque, which makes it the right choice for holes and for anything requiring control. The impact driver hammers rotationally through a hex collet, producing two to three times the torque with almost no kickback.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy the drill first. It is the versatile one, it does the job an impact driver genuinely cannot, and its clutch protects your work while you learn. Add the impact driver when long screws, decking or framing enter your life — at which point you will wonder how you managed without it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two habits make owning both far more pleasant: buy impact-rated bits for everything, since they work in both tools, and keep a drill bit loaded in one and a driver bit in the other. That is where most of the time saving actually comes from.
          </p>
